      "updated": 1786379597840,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Flink Task Managers",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Flink Task Managers",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Y7-RQHn5ZUBViP7CEDFO7",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 4923.506517297864,
      "y": 285.87897381472976,
      "width": 790.679443359375,
      "height": 250,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5M",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 60778816,
      "version": 107,
      "versionNonce": 623952576,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1786379597840,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "For a million events per second, I would first design Kafka with sufficient\npartitions so events can be processed in parallel. The partition key would likely\nbe user_id because most features are user-level aggregates and we want all\nevents for a user to maintain ordering. On the Flink side, I would increase\noperator parallelism and distribute state across task managers. I would monitor\nKafka consumer lag, Flink backpressure, checkpoint duration, and feature store\nlatency. If lag increases, I would identify whether the bottleneck is ingestion,\nprocessing, or storage and scale the relevant component. For a fintech system, I\nwould also ensure fault tolerance using Flink checkpoints and exactly-once\nprocessing to avoid incorrect feature calculations.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "For a million events per second, I would first design Kafka with sufficient partitions so events can be processed in parallel. The partition key would likely be user_id because most features are user-level aggregates and we want all events for a user to maintain ordering. On the Flink side, I would increase operator parallelism and distribute state across task managers. I would monitor Kafka consumer lag, Flink backpressure, checkpoint duration, and feature store latency. If lag increases, I would identify whether the bottleneck is ingestion, processing, or storage and scale the relevant component. For a fintech system, I would also ensure fault tolerance using Flink checkpoints and exactly-once processing to avoid incorrect feature calculations.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "aQNzRVT4mTuX0BE-2f1HU",
      "type": "rectangle",
      "x": 4891.912645227551,
      "y": -444.59758868527024,
      "width": 904.4140625,
      "height": 1017.66796875,
